
# Day 7 - Character Classification

## 1. Check if the Sentence Is Pangram
- **Technique:** Character Tracking using Set
- **Approach:** Scan every character and manually convert uppercase letters into lowercase using ASCII values. Store each English alphabet character in a set and check whether all 26 letters are present.
- **Time Complexity:** O(n)
- **Auxiliary Space:** O(1)
- **Edge Case:** A sentence with all letters except one must return False.

## 2. Number of Segments in a String
- **Technique:** String Traversal and Boundary Checking
- **Approach:** Traverse the string and identify the beginning of each segment. A segment starts when a non-space character appears at index 0 or immediately after a space.
- **Time Complexity:** O(n)
- **Auxiliary Space:** O(1)
- **Edge Case:** An empty string or a string containing only spaces returns 0.

## 3. Count Vowels and Consonants
- **Technique:** Manual Character Classification
- **Approach:** Traverse the input and identify English letters using character-range comparisons. Convert uppercase letters manually and classify each alphabet character as either a vowel or consonant.
- **Time Complexity:** O(n)
- **Auxiliary Space:** O(1)
- **Edge Case:** A string containing only numbers and special characters returns zero vowels and zero consonants.

## Learning Outcomes
- Applied ASCII-based character identification.
- Practised manual uppercase-to-lowercase conversion.
- Understood set-based alphabet tracking.
- Learned to detect segment boundaries during traversal.
- Classified vowels and consonants without built-in shortcuts.
- Improved string traversal and conditional logic.
